<h1>Bcrypt Password Hashing: A Practical Beginner Guide</h1>
<p>Bcrypt is a password hashing algorithm designed for a different job than MD5 or SHA-256. General-purpose hashes are fast. Bcrypt is intentionally slower and adjustable. That makes it better for storing passwords, because an attacker who obtains a password database must spend more time testing each guess.</p>
<p>The key idea in bcrypt is the work factor, often called cost. A higher cost makes hashing slower. Developers choose a cost that is acceptable for real logins but expensive for large offline attacks. OWASP lists bcrypt as an option for legacy systems and recommends a work factor of 10 or more in its <a href="https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Password_Storage_Cheat_Sheet.html">Password Storage Cheat Sheet</a>.</p>
<p>Bcrypt also includes salt handling. A salt is a unique random value used with each password. It means two users with the same password should not end up with the same stored hash. Most bcrypt libraries store the algorithm marker, cost, salt, and hash in one string. That is useful because verification later needs the same parameters.</p>
<p>A common mistake is to hash the password manually with MD5 or SHA-256 before bcrypt. Do not do this unless your framework specifically documents a safe reason. Pre-hashing can introduce encoding problems, length problems, and migration confusion. Use a trusted library and pass the user's password to the password hashing function as intended.</p>
<p>Bcrypt has an important limitation: many implementations process only the first 72 bytes of the password. OWASP notes this input limit. For most normal passwords this may not matter, but it does matter for passphrases, international characters, and systems that allow very long passwords. Frameworks may handle this differently, so read the library documentation before making assumptions.</p>
<p>In January, many teams review old security tasks after year-end freezes. Password hashing is a good candidate for that review. Check whether your application uses MD5, SHA-1, plain SHA-256, or a home-built salt format. If it does, plan a migration. Bcrypt can be a practical target when Argon2id is not available in your environment.</p>
<p>Bcrypt is not the newest option. Argon2id is often preferred when available because it can be memory-hard as well as time-costly. Still, bcrypt remains widely supported, tested, and available in many frameworks. For teams maintaining older stacks, it can be a major improvement over fast legacy hashes.</p>
<p>When choosing a bcrypt cost, measure it on your own production-like hardware. A setting that feels fine on a developer laptop may behave differently under real login traffic, and a setting that is too low may age badly as hardware improves. Store the full bcrypt hash string so future code can read the cost and upgrade it when needed.</p>
<p>Review the library documentation for your language before deployment. Bcrypt implementations are common, but details such as input length, encoding, cost syntax, and error handling can differ. A small test suite with known valid and invalid passwords helps catch migration errors before users are affected.</p>
<p>The basic rule is clear: use bcrypt through a well-maintained password library, set a cost your system can handle, rely on the library's salt handling, and store the full hash string. Do not use MD5 for passwords. If you are still deciding between concepts, review <a href="/why-md5-is-bad-for-passwords.html">Why MD5 Is Bad for Passwords</a> first.</p>
